.TH tutee n 0.3 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tutee
.PP
\fBtutee\fR provides tee\-like helpers in pure Tcl.
.SS Package
.CS
package require tclutils::tutee 0.3
.CE
.SS Commands
.CS
::tclutils::tutee::write data paths ?-append bool? ?-stdout bool? ?-nonewline bool?
::tclutils::tutee::writeFile inputFile paths ?-append bool? ?-stdout bool?
::tclutils::tutee::copyChannel inChan outChans ?-stdout bool? ?-chunksize n?
.CE
.SS Options
.CS
-append bool      append instead of overwrite (write, writeFile; default 0)
-stdout bool      also write to stdout (default 0)
-nonewline bool   write only: no newline at the end (default 0)
-chunksize n      copyChannel only: characters per read (default 8192)
.CE
.PP
\fBwrite\fR writes \fBdata\fR with \fBputs\fR, so a \fB\en\fR is appended unless \fB\-nonewline 1\fR (unlike the shell \fBtee\fR, which adds nothing); it returns \fBdata\fR. \fBwriteFile\fR copies the bytes of \fBinputFile\fR unchanged, CR and encoding included, and returns the number of bytes (0.3; 0.2 read it as text, wrote LF in the system encoding and returned the text). \fBcopyChannel\fR returns the number of characters copied (bytes for binary channels); the caller opens and closes the channels.
.PP
\fBwrite\fR (and \fBwriteFile\fR) open every target before anything is written, without truncating. Only when all targets are open are they emptied (unless \fB\-append 1\fR). If a target cannot be opened, the channels already opened are closed, files created by this call are removed again, existing files keep their content, and the error of \fBopen\fR is raised (e.g. \fBPOSIX ENOENT ...\fR).
.PP
Option errors: \fBTCLUTILS COMMON BOOLEAN \-name\fR, \fBTCLUTILS COMMON INTEGER \-chunksize\fR, \fBTCLUTILS COMMON OPTION \-name\fR.
.SS Example
.CS
::tclutils::tutee::write "hello" {a.txt b.txt}
::tclutils::tutee::writeFile input.txt {copy1.txt copy2.txt}
.CE
